```{r setup, include=FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  echo = TRUE,
  eval = greta:::check_tf_version("message"),
  cache = FALSE,
  comment = NA,
  progress = FALSE
)
library(greta)
```

This page shows how to get the same results from greta twice: what `set.seed()` fixes, what else has to stay the same, and how that compares with other probabilistic programming languages.

In short: call `set.seed()` before `mcmc()` or `calculate()`, and keep the other arguments and the future plan the same.

## Seeding `mcmc()`

`set.seed()` is all `mcmc()` needs. greta draws its own seed from R's random number generator and gives it to the sampler, so the same seed fixes both the initial values and every draw.

```{r mcmc_seed}
x <- normal(0, 1)
m <- model(x)

set.seed(2026 - 09 - 27)
one <- mcmc(m, warmup = 100, n_samples = 100, chains = 2, verbose = FALSE)

set.seed(2026 - 09 - 27)
two <- mcmc(m, warmup = 100, n_samples = 100, chains = 2, verbose = FALSE)

identical(as.matrix(one), as.matrix(two))
```

Without a new `set.seed()`, the next run continues from where R's random number stream got to, so it gives different draws, just as `rnorm()` would:

```{r mcmc_stream}
three <- mcmc(m, warmup = 100, n_samples = 100, chains = 2, verbose = FALSE)
identical(as.matrix(two), as.matrix(three))
```

`tensorflow::set_random_seed()` sets R's seed too, so it gives the same draws as `set.seed()` with the same number:

```{r set_random_seed}
tensorflow::set_random_seed(2026 - 09 - 27, disable_gpu = FALSE)
four <- mcmc(m, warmup = 100, n_samples = 100, chains = 2, verbose = FALSE)
identical(as.matrix(one), as.matrix(four))
```

Pass `disable_gpu = FALSE`. Without it, `set_random_seed()` hides the GPU for the rest of the R session, including from later runs that ask for one with `compute_options = gpu_only()`.

## Seeding `calculate()`

`calculate()` takes a `seed` argument, which fixes its draws without touching R's random number stream:

```{r calculate_seed}
y <- normal(0, 1)
a <- calculate(y, nsim = 3, seed = 1)
b <- calculate(y, nsim = 3, seed = 1)
identical(a, b)
```

It also respects `set.seed()`, like `mcmc()`:

```{r calculate_set_seed}
set.seed(1)
c1 <- calculate(y, nsim = 3)
set.seed(1)
c2 <- calculate(y, nsim = 3)
identical(c1, c2)
```

## What else has to stay the same

A seed fixes the random numbers. To get the same draws, the sampler also has to use them in the same way, and a few arguments change that.

### Progress updates

`mcmc()` runs the sampler in bursts, returning to R between them to update the progress bar. Each burst takes its own random numbers, so changing where the bursts break changes the draws. `verbose`, `pb_update` and `one_by_one` all do that:

```{r verbose, message = FALSE, results = "hide"}
set.seed(2026 - 09 - 27)
quiet <- mcmc(m, warmup = 100, n_samples = 100, chains = 2, verbose = FALSE)

set.seed(2026 - 09 - 27)
chatty <- mcmc(m, warmup = 100, n_samples = 100, chains = 2)
```

```{r verbose_differs}
identical(as.matrix(quiet), as.matrix(chatty))
```

Both runs are equally valid samples from the posterior; they are just not the same sample. To repeat a run exactly, repeat these arguments as well as the seed.

### Parallel chains and the future plan

greta runs chains in parallel with the [future](https://future.futureverse.org/) package. Under the same plan, with the same number of workers, seeded runs are reproducible:

```{r parallel_same}
future::plan(future::multisession, workers = 2)

set.seed(2026 - 09 - 27)
par_one <- mcmc(m, warmup = 100, n_samples = 100, chains = 2, verbose = FALSE)

set.seed(2026 - 09 - 27)
par_two <- mcmc(m, warmup = 100, n_samples = 100, chains = 2, verbose = FALSE)

identical(as.matrix(par_one), as.matrix(par_two))
```

`extra_samples()` carries each chain on from where it stopped, with new random numbers, whether it runs in parallel or sequentially:

```{r extra_samples}
more <- extra_samples(par_one, n_samples = 100, verbose = FALSE)
coda::niter(more)
```

But changing the plan, or its number of workers, changes the draws. greta splits chains between samplers by the number of workers: sequentially, every chain is in one sampler, while with two workers each chain gets its own. Each sampler has its own seed, so the grouping decides which random numbers each chain gets:

```{r parallel_differs}
future::plan(future::sequential)

set.seed(2026 - 09 - 27)
seq_one <- mcmc(m, warmup = 100, n_samples = 100, chains = 2, verbose = FALSE)

identical(as.matrix(par_one), as.matrix(seq_one))
```

So record the plan and its number of workers alongside the seed. A run with `plan(multisession)` and no `workers` argument uses as many workers as the machine has cores, which differs between machines: set `workers` explicitly if the run needs to be repeated elsewhere.

`hmc()`, the default sampler, also differs between plans with a single chain, because it chooses its number of leapfrog steps from R's random numbers, and future gives each worker its own stream.

## How this compares with other software

The behaviour on this page follows two conventions that are standard in probabilistic programming:

- **Seeding comes from the user's session, not hidden global state.** `set.seed()` reaches the sampler, and nothing in TensorFlow's own global state affects the draws. PyMC makes the same choice: [`pymc.sample()`](https://www.pymc.io/projects/docs/en/stable/api/generated/pymc.sample.html) ignores global seeding and takes its seed as an argument.
- **Continuing a chain continues its random numbers.** A chain resumed with `extra_samples()` draws new random numbers wherever it runs, rather than replaying the ones it has already used.

In one respect greta does not yet match that standard. In [Stan](https://mc-stan.org/docs/reference-manual/reproducibility.html), a chain's random numbers are fixed by the seed and the chain's index, so the same seed gives the same chains however they are run. PyMC seeds each chain separately for the same reason, and the future package guarantees that [random numbers do not depend on the backend or the number of workers](https://henrikbengtsson.github.io/future-tutorial-user2022/random-numbers-and-reproducibility.html). greta seeds each sampler rather than each chain, which is why the plan matters above. Seeding each chain by its index is the behaviour we are working towards, in greta-dev/greta#XXX.

Stan's reproducibility guide also makes a point that applies to greta: identical results need the same software as well as the same seed. Different versions of greta, TensorFlow or TensorFlow Probability can give different draws from the same seed, as can a different operating system or processor.

## GPUs

A seed fixes the random numbers, not the arithmetic. On a GPU, some TensorFlow operations add numbers up in an order that varies from run to run, so results can differ slightly between runs with the same seed. To make GPU runs repeatable, turn on TensorFlow's deterministic operations before sampling, at some cost in speed:

```{r gpu, eval = FALSE}
tensorflow::tf$config$experimental$enable_op_determinism()
```

Runs on the CPU, greta's default, do not need this.
